>>
endobj
93 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 72 0 R
/E (A growing body of research highlights the potential of micro-break activities as an effective intervention within both professional and educational settings. Short, movement-based breaks, such as stretching, walking, or performing light mobility exercises, have been shown to improve circulation, reduce fatigue, and mitigate musculoskeletal strain. Moreover, these brief physical activities enhance cognitive performance and alleviate mental fatigue, indicating a dual benefit for both body and mind. \(Albulescu et al., 2022\) )
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
94 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H4
/P 72 0 R
/T (In Workplace Settings: )
/E (In Workplace Settings: )
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
95 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 72 0 R
/E (Sedentary work and prolonged sitting are established risk factors for declining health and productivity. A 2022 study published in Scientific Reports demonstrated that incorporating micro-exercise routines in the workplace effectively reduces long-term sickness absence \(LTSA\). When performed collectively, these brief activities support both physical well-being \(e.g., reducing pain\) and psychosocial health, offering a holistic approach to workplace wellness. \(Radwan et al., 2022\). Another study from 2023 \(Impact of active breaks on sedentary behaviour and perception of productivity in office workers\) highlights how reduction of sedentary behaviour \(influenced by encouraged activity breaks\) decreases the perceived stress, sleepiness, and simultaneously increases quality of life among productivity. \(Batista-Ferreira et al., 2024\)  )
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
